 power plants to meet lighting demand.\r\n442: \r\n443: The scheme originally targeted distributing 400 million CFLs across India. Later, the focus shifted from CFLs to LED bulbs under the UJALA scheme launched in 2015.\r\n444: \r\n445: **Q: Discuss the significance of UNFCCC in addressing global climate change challenges. (4 marks)**\r\n446: \r\n447: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. Currently, 198 countries have ratified the Convention. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system. The Convention follows the principle of \"common but differentiated responsibilities\" meaning all countries share the responsibility of fighting climate change, but developed countries should take the lead since they have historically contributed more to greenhouse gas emissions.\r\n448: \r\n449: **Q: Write a short note on climate change and development of UNFCCC. (7 marks)**\r\n450: \r\n451: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system.\r\n452: \r\n453: Key principles of UNFCCC:\r\n454: 1. Common but differentiated responsibilities: Developed countries should lead in reducing emissions.\r\n455: 2. Precautionary principle: Lack of full scientific certainty should not be used as a reason to delay action.\r\n456: 3. Right to sustainable development: Countries have the right to promote sustainable development.\r\n457: 4. Supportive and open international economic system: Trade measures should not be used as barriers.\r\n458: \r\n459: Classification of countries:\r\n460: 1. Annex I: Developed countries and economies in transition including USA, EU, Japan, Russia, and Australia.\r\n461: 2. Annex II: Subset of Annex I wealthy developed countries required to provide financial and technical support to developing countries.\r\n462: 3. Non-Annex I: Developing countries including India, China, and Brazil.\r\n463: \r\n464: Major outcomes and agreements under UNFCCC:\r\n465: 1. Kyoto Protocol (1997): First legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries.\r\n466: 2. Copenhagen Accord (2009): Voluntary pledges.\r\n467: 3. Paris Agreement (2015): All countries agreed to limit warming to 2Â°C with Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs).\r\n468: \r\n469: UNFCCC operates through annual meetings called Conference of Parties (COP). The COP is the supreme decision making body of the Convention. UNFCCC created the framework for all subsequent climate agreements. Without UNFCCC, there would be no Kyoto Protocol, no Paris Agreement, and no CDM mechanism.\r\n470: \r\n471: **Q: Explain the principles and objectives of the Kyoto Protocol. Assess the effectiveness of the Kyoto Protocol in reducing greenhouse gas emissions. (4 marks)**\r\n472: \r\n473: The Kyoto Protocol is an international agreement linked to UNFCCC that was adopted in Kyoto, Japan on 11 December 1997 and came into force on 16 February 2005.\r\n474: \r\n475: Key features:\r\n476: 1. Set legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries. First commitment period was 2008 to 2012 to reduce GHG emissions by an average of 5.2 percent below 1990 levels. Second commitment period was 2013 to 2020 via Doha Amendment.\r\n477: 2. Covers six greenhouse gases: CO2, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6.\r\n478: 3. Has three flexible mechanisms to help countries meet targets: CDM, Joint Implementation, and Emissions Trading.\r\n479: 4. Developing countries including India and China have no binding targets.\r\n480: 5. USA never ratified the Protocol and Canada withdrew in 2012.\r\n481: \r\n482: Effectiveness: The Protocol succeeded in reducing emissions in many developed countries. The EU met their collective target. However, global emissions continued to rise because major emitters like USA, China, and India were not bound by targets. This led to the Paris Agreement in 2015 which covers all countries.\r\n483: \r\n484: **Q: Explain Sustainable development. (3 marks)**\r\n485: \r\n486: Sustainable development is defined as development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. It rests on three pillars: economic growth, social inclusion, and environmental protection. In the context of energy, sustainable development means using energy resources efficiently, switching to renewable sources, and reducing pollution and greenhouse gas emissions while maintaining economic growth.\r\n487: \r\n488: **Q: What is the Prototype Carbon Fund? What are its objectives? State the three primary strategic objectives of PCF. (3 marks)**\r\n489: \r\n490: The Prototype Carbon Fund (PCF) was launched by the World Bank in April 2000 with capital of US$ 180 million contributed by 6 governments and 17 private companies. PCF was the first carbon fund in the world. It was designed to demonstrate how project based greenhouse gas emission reductions under the Kyoto Protocol CDM and JI mechanisms could work in practice.\r\n491: \r\n492: Three primary objectives of PCF:\r\n493: 1. To show how project based transactions for greenhouse gas emission reductions can contribute to sustainable development and lower the cost of compliance with Kyoto Protocol targets.\r\n494: 2. To provide a learning experience for stakeholders on how the CDM and JI mechanisms work in practice.\r\n495: 3. To demonstrate how the World Bank can work as a financial intermediary in the carbon market, acting as trustee of the fund and purchasing emission reductions on behalf of contributors.\r\n496: \r\n497: PCF invested in 23 projects in developing countries and economies in transition covering renewable energy, energy efficiency, and waste management.\r\n498: \r\n499: **Q: State the roles and responsibilities of COP (Conference of the Parties). (4 marks)**\r\n500: \r\n501: COP stands for Conference of the Parties. It is the supreme decision making body of the UNFCCC. All countries that have ratified the Convention are represented at COP. COP meets annually in different cities around the world.\r\n502: \r\n503: Roles and responsibilities:\r\n504: 1. Review the implementation of the Convention and its related legal instruments.\r\n505: 2. Make decisions necessary to promote effective implementation of the Convention.\r\n506: 3. Adopt new protocols, amendments, or annexes to the Convention.\r\n507: 4. Review national communications and emission inventories submitted by Parties.\r\n508: 5. Assess the overall effects of measures taken by Parties to address climate change.\r\n509: 6. Mobilize financial resources for climate action in developing countries.\r\n510: 7. Review reports from subsidiary bodies.\r\n511: \r\n512: Notable COPs include COP 3 in Kyoto for the Kyoto Protocol, COP 15 in Copenhagen for the Copenhagen Accord, COP 21 in Paris for the Paris Agreement, and COP 26 in Glasgow for the Glasgow Climate Pact.\r\n513: \r\n514: **Q: Explain the Carbon Credit Trade Scheme. (3-4 marks)**\r\n515: \r\n516: A carbon credit is a tradable permit or certificate that allows the holder to emit one tonne of CO2 or an equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas.\r\n517: \r\n518: How the scheme works:\r\n519: 1. A regulatory body sets a cap on total emissions allowed.\r\n520: 2. Emission allowances are distributed or auctioned to industries.\r\n521: 3. Companies that reduce their emissions below their allowance have surplus credits.\r\n522: 4. Companies that emit more than their allowance must buy credits from those with surplus.\r\n523: 5. This creates a market based mechanism where the price of carbon is determined by supply and demand.\r\n524: 6. The system rewards companies that invest in clean technology and penalizes those that continue polluting.\r\n525: \r\n526: Types of carbon markets include compliance markets created by mandatory regulations and voluntary markets where companies voluntarily buy carbon credits to offset their emissions.\r\n527: \r\n528: India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) was notified in June 2023 under the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022. BEE is the administrator. It covers obligated entities in designated sectors. India plans to have a fully operational carbon market by 2026.\r\n529: \r\n530: **Q: Explain the Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme. (3-4 marks)**\r\n531: \r\n532: PAT is a market based energy efficiency trading mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency. This is one of the eight missions under India's National Action Plan on Climate Change.\r\n533: \r\n534: How PAT works:\r\n535: 1. BEE identifies Designated Consumers (DCs) in energy intensive sectors such as thermal power plants, iron and steel, cement, aluminum, fertilizers, textile, pulp and paper, chlor alkali, railways, refineries, petrochemicals, distribution companies, and buildings.\r\n536: 2. Each DC is given a Specific Energy Consumption (SEC) reduction target based on their current performance.\r\n537: 3. DCs that reduce their SEC beyond the target earn Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts).\r\n538: 4. DCs that fail to meet their target must buy ESCerts from the market or pay a penalty.\r\n539: 5. ESCerts are traded on two power exchanges: Indian Energy Exchange and Power Exchange India Limited.\r\n540: \r\n541: PAT Cycle I covered 478 designated consumers and achieved energy savings of 8.67 million tonnes of oil equivalent against a target of 6.686 MTOE. This exceeded the target by about 30 percent. PAT is currently in its 7th cycle, covering over 1000 designated consumers across 13 sectors.\r\n542: \r\n543: **Question: Write a note on CDM methodology and its project cycle with neat sketch. (7 marks)**\r\n544: \r\n545: **What to draw:** A vertical or circular flowchart with 7 boxes connected by arrows:\r\n546: Project Design (PDD) -> National Approval (DNA) -> Validation (DOE) -> Registration (CDM Executive Board) -> Monitoring (Project Developer) -> Verification (Second DOE) -> CER Issuance (CDM Executive Board)\r\n547: \r\n548: **Google search:** `CDM project cycle flowchart diagram`\r\n549: \r\n550: **Question: Explain ball float steam trap with neat sketch. (Part of 7-mark steam trap question)**\r\n551: \r\n552: **What to draw:** Cross-section showing: trap body (chamber), inlet pipe on left, outlet pipe on right-bottom, a hollow ball floating on condensate, valve seat connected to the ball by a lever, vent at top for air release.\r\n553: \r\n554: **How it works in the diagram:** When condensate fills the body, ball rises and opens valve. When steam enters, ball drops and closes valve.\r\n555: \r\n556: **Google search:** `ball float steam trap working diagram`\r\n557: \r\n558: **Question: Explain inverted bucket steam trap with neat sketch. (Part of 7-mark steam trap question)**\r\n559: \r\n560: **What to draw:** Cross-section showing: trap body, an inverted bucket (open end down) inside the body, inlet pipe entering at bottom into the bucket, outlet valve at top connected to bucket by a lever, outlet pipe.\r\n561: \r\n562: **How it works in the diagram:** Steam enters bucket from below, bucket floats up, closes valve. Steam condenses, bucket sinks, valve opens, condensate drains out.\r\n563: \r\n564: **Google search:** `inverted bucket steam trap working diagram`\r\n565: \r\n566: **Question: This is not a specific PYQ diagram, but drawing a pie chart or bar chart of boiler losses helps in the indirect method question.**\r\n567: \r\n568: **What to draw:** A simple pie chart showing typical losses for an oil-fired boiler:\r\n569: - Dry flue gas loss: 7-8%\r\n570: - Hydrogen in fuel: 7%\r\n571: - Moisture losses: 0.5%\r\n572: - Radiation: 2%\r\n573: - Useful heat output: 82-83%\r\n574: \r\n575: \r\n576: *End of Study Guide*\r\n577: \r\n578: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
