{"step_index":28,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T08:09:10Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T13:39:10+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T13:39:10+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/college/College%20sem%205/ecm/ECM_QA_Continuous.md`\nTotal Lines: 165\nTotal Bytes: 45285\nShowing lines 1 to 165\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: **Q: Discuss the salient features of Energy Conservation Act 2001. (7 marks)**\r\n2: \r\n3: The Energy Conservation Act was enacted in October 2001 in India. Its primary objective is to reduce the energy intensity of the Indian economy. The government has notified a list of designated consumers in 9 sectors, BEE star labeling, and ECBC norms under this act. Key features of the Act are: 1. The Central Government can specify energy consumption standards for appliances and equipment. 2. It mandates standards and labeling for equipment and appliances. 3. Designated consumers (large energy users) must appoint an energy manager, get energy audits done, and comply with norms. 4. It established the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) under the Ministry of Power. 5. It defines penalties for non-compliance with the rules. 6. It introduced the Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) for commercial buildings. 7. State Designated Agencies (SDAs) are established for implementation at the state level. 8. The Central Government can prohibit the manufacture or sale of equipment not meeting standards. 9. It sets energy consumption standards for notified equipment. 10. It requires the mandatory display of energy consumption labels on equipment.\r\n4: \r\n5: **Q: Enlist the schemes of Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) and discuss any two in detail. (7 marks)**\r\n6: \r\n7: The schemes of the Bureau of Energy Efficiency include: 1. Standards and Labeling Programme 2. Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) 3. Designated Consumers Programme 4. Demand Side Management (DSM) 5. Bachat Lamp Yojna 6. SME Energy Efficiency Programme 7. Agriculture DSM Programme 8. Municipal DSM Programme 9. National Mission on Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE) 10. Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) Scheme Standards and Labeling Programme provides star ratings for appliances based on their energy consumption. It helps consumers make informed choices by showing the energy efficiency of a product. Products with higher star ratings consume less electricity and save money over time. Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC) sets minimum energy performance standards for new commercial buildings. It applies to buildings with a connected load of 100 kW or more. It covers aspects like the building envelope, HVAC, and lighting to ensure the building consumes less energy compared to conventional designs.\r\n8: \r\n9: **Q: Enlist various schemes of BEE and its benefits. (3 marks)**\r\n10: \r\n11: The BEE schemes include the Standards and Labeling Programme, ECBC, PAT Scheme, Demand Side Management, and Bachat Lamp Yojna. These schemes help reduce energy consumption, lower electricity bills for consumers, and decrease the peak power demand on the grid. They also promote the use of energy-efficient appliances and sustainable building designs across the country.\r\n12: \r\n13: **Q: Write a short note on Indian Energy Scenario. (7 marks)**\r\n14: \r\n15: *(Note: For a 4-mark question \"Discuss sector-wise energy consumption scenario in India\", write only the first two paragraphs.)* India is the third largest energy consumer globally after China and the USA. The primary energy sources heavily rely on coal which meets about 55% of the demand. Oil accounts for about 30% and natural gas for about 8%. Hydro, nuclear, and renewables make up the rest. Sector-wise consumption shows Industry at the top with roughly 45% usage. Transport uses about 18%, Residential 23%, Agriculture 8%, and Commercial 6%. Coal dominates power generation and produces about 70% of the country's electricity. India faces a high import dependency. About 80% of crude oil and 50% of natural gas are imported. The growing population, rapid urbanization, and industrial growth continuously increase energy demand. Despite total high consumption, the per capita energy consumption is still low. India's per capita consumption is about 600 kgoe compared to the world average of 1800 kgoe. The government is heavily pushing for renewables to meet future demand. Initiatives like the National Solar Mission and a target of 500 GW of renewable energy capacity by 2030 are key steps in this direction.\r\n16: \r\n17: **Q: Define energy security. Enlist different strategies to achieve it. (3 marks)**\r\n18: \r\n19: Energy security means the continuous availability of energy in various forms, in sufficient quantities, and at reasonable prices. Strategies to achieve energy security include: 1. Diversifying energy sources. 2. Increasing domestic energy production. 3. Building strategic energy reserves. 4. Promoting renewable energy. 5. Implementing energy conservation measures. 6. Engaging in international energy cooperation.\r\n20: \r\n21: **Q: Define energy security. Enlist strategies and discuss the role of energy conservation in achieving energy security. (7 marks)**\r\n22: \r\n23: Energy security means the continuous availability of energy in various forms, in sufficient quantities, and at reasonable prices. Strategies to achieve it include diversifying energy sources, increasing domestic production, building strategic reserves, promoting renewables, implementing energy conservation, and international cooperation. Energy conservation plays a major role in achieving energy security. It directly reduces the total energy demand of the country. Lower demand means the country needs to import fewer fossil fuels like oil and gas. This reduces the financial burden and improves economic stability. Conservation also extends the life of existing domestic fossil fuel reserves. Using less energy lowers environmental impact and greenhouse gas emissions.\r\n24: \r\n25: **Q: Define: (i) Renewable Energy (ii) Non-Renewable Energy (iii) Per Capita Energy Consumption. (3 marks)**\r\n26: \r\n27: Renewable Energy comes from sources that are naturally replenished. Examples include solar, wind, hydro, biomass, and geothermal energy. Non-Renewable Energy comes from sources that get depleted and cannot be replenished in a human timescale. Examples include coal, oil, natural gas, and nuclear energy. Per Capita Energy Consumption is the total energy consumed by a country divided by its population. India's per capita consumption is about 600 kgoe per year.\r\n28: \r\n29: **Q: Differentiate between primary and secondary sources and give examples. (4 marks)**\r\n30: \r\n31: Primary sources are found directly in nature and used without conversion. Examples include coal, crude oil, natural gas, solar radiation, and wind. Secondary sources are derived from primary sources through a conversion process. Examples include electricity, petrol, diesel, LPG, and coke.\r\n32: \r\n33: **Q: Define Energy Conservation and explain its importance. (4 marks)**\r\n34: \r\n35: Energy conservation means reducing energy consumption by using less energy for the same work output or getting more work from the same amount of energy. The importance of energy conservation includes: 1. It reduces energy bills for industries and households. 2. It decreases dependence on imported fuels like oil and gas. 3. It reduces environmental pollution and greenhouse gas emissions. 4. It extends the life of existing energy resources. 5. It improves the competitiveness of Indian industry in the global market. 6. It reduces the need for new power plant construction. Example calculation: Replacing a 100W incandescent bulb with a 10W LED saves 90W. For a factory with 1000 such bulbs running 10 hours a day, the annual saving is (1000 * 90 * 10 * 365) / 1000 = 3,28,500 kWh.\r\n36: \r\n37: **Q: Define ECBC and its significance in promoting energy-efficient building construction. (7 marks)**\r\n38: \r\n39: ECBC stands for Energy Conservation Building Code. It was launched by BEE in 2007 and updated in 2017 as ECBC 2017. The code applies to commercial buildings with a connected load of 100 kW or more, or a contract demand of 120 kVA or more. It covers 6 building systems. These are the building envelope, HVAC, lighting, electrical systems, water heating, and renewable energy. It sets minimum energy performance standards for new commercial buildings. There are three levels of compliance: ECBC as the baseline, ECBC+ for enhanced efficiency, and SuperECBC for near net-zero energy buildings. ECBC buildings consume 25 to 50 percent less energy than conventional buildings. States have the power to adopt and notify the ECBC with modifications to suit local climatic conditions. It is currently voluntary at the national level, but states like Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, UP, and Rajasthan have made it mandatory.\r\n40: \r\n41: **Q: Discuss the provisions of Electricity Act 2003 related to energy conservation. (7 marks)**\r\n42: \r\n43: The Electricity Act was enacted in 2003. It replaced the Indian Electricity Act 1910, the Electricity Supply Act 1948, and the Electricity Regulatory Commissions Act 1998. Section 3 states that the national electricity policy and plan must include provisions for energy conservation. Section 61 requires tariff regulations to promote efficiency in electricity use. Section 86(1)(e) directs state commissions to promote cogeneration and renewable energy sources. Section 166 advises state governments to promote energy efficiency. The Act also delicensed power generation except for hydro projects above 25 MW and nuclear power. It introduced open access provisions to promote competition and reduce transmission losses. These provisions increased private participation, reduced transmission and distribution losses, and promoted competition.\r\n44: \r\n45: **Q: List the missions under the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC). (3 marks)**\r\n46: \r\n47: The NAPCC was launched by India in June 2008. It has 8 National Missions: 1. National Solar Mission 2. National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE) 3. National Mission on Sustainable Habitat 4. National Water Mission 5. National Mission for Sustaining the Himalayan Ecosystem 6. National Mission for a Green India 7. National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture 8. National Mission on Strategic Knowledge for Climate Change\r\n48: \r\n49: **Q: Explain in brief energy pricing. (3 marks)**\r\n50: \r\n51: Energy pricing is the mechanism by which energy costs are determined for consumers. In India, energy prices are set through a mix of market forces and government regulation. Electricity tariffs are set by State Electricity Regulatory Commissions (SERCs) and vary by consumer category such as domestic, commercial, industrial, and agricultural. Petroleum product prices were deregulated between 2010 and 2014 and now follow international crude oil prices. Natural gas prices are set by the government every 6 months using a formula linked to international gas prices. Coal prices are set by Coal India Ltd with government oversight. Energy pricing directly affects conservation behavior because higher prices encourage energy saving. There is also cross-subsidization where industrial and commercial consumers pay higher rates to subsidize domestic and agricultural consumers.\r\n52: \r\n53: **Q: Enlist various energy conservation techniques for industrial furnaces. (3 marks)**\r\n54: \r\n55: 1. Complete combustion with minimum excess air 2. Correct amount of air for combustion 3. Preheating combustion air using waste heat from flue gases 4. Preheating the charge or load material before placing in furnace 5. Proper insulation and refractory maintenance to reduce wall losses 6. Waste heat recovery from flue gases 7. Controlling furnace draft properly 8. Regular maintenance and calibration of burners\r\n56: \r\n57: **Q: Write recommendations for efficient design of furnace. (4 marks)**\r\n58: \r\n59: 1. Complete combustion with minimum excess air. Excess air beyond what is needed carries away heat. 2. Preheating combustion air. Using recuperators to preheat air with flue gas heat saves about 1% fuel for every 20Â°C rise. 3. Preheating the charge. Preheating materials using waste flue gases directly reduces the energy needed to reach operating temperatures. 4. Proper insulation. Using ceramic fiber insulation on furnace walls and doors reduces heat leakage. 5. Controlling furnace draft. Proper draft control prevents air infiltration and incomplete combustion. 6. Waste heat recovery. Use waste heat boilers or regenerators to recover heat from hot flue gases. 7. Efficient burner operation. Ensure regular maintenance and clean nozzles for proper air to fuel ratios.\r\n60: \r\n61: **Q: Discuss general fuel economy measures in furnaces. (7 marks)**\r\n62: \r\n63: 1. Complete combustion with minimum excess air: Excess air beyond what is needed for complete combustion carries away heat. Typical excess air should be 10 to 25% depending on fuel type. Monitor O2 in flue gas. 2. Preheating combustion air: For every 20Â°C rise in combustion air temperature, fuel savings of about 1% can be achieved. Use recuperators or regenerators to preheat air using flue gas heat. 3. Preheating the charge: If the charge is preheated using waste flue gases, the furnace needs less energy to bring it to operating temperature. This directly reduces fuel consumption. 4. Controlling furnace draft: Excessive draft increases air infiltration and heat loss. Insufficient draft causes incomplete combustion. Use dampers and draft gauges. 5. Proper insulation: Use ceramic fiber insulation on furnace walls and doors. Every crack or opening leaks heat. Maintain refractory lining regularly. 6. Waste heat recovery from flue gases: Use recuperators, regenerators, or waste heat boilers to recover heat from hot flue gases. Flue gas temperature above 750Â°C has significant recoverable heat. 7. Optimum temperature control: Avoid overheating. Every 10Â°C rise above required temperature increases fuel consumption by about 1 to 2%. 8. Reducing heat stored in furnace structure: Use low thermal mass materials like ceramic fiber instead of firebrick for furnace walls. This reduces heat stored in walls during heating cycles. 9. Minimizing wall losses: Reduce openings, use properly sized doors, and keep doors closed when not loading or unloading. 10. Efficient burner operation: Ensure regular maintenance, proper air to fuel ratio, and clean nozzles.\r\n64: \r\n65: **Q: Define boiler efficiency. Explain direct and indirect methods of boiler efficiency evaluation. (3 marks / 4 marks)**\r\n66: \r\n67: Boiler efficiency is the ratio of heat output (heat absorbed by steam) to heat input (heat content of fuel fired). Evaporation Ratio is the ratio of steam generated per kg of fuel consumed. ER = Steam generation (kg/hr) / Fuel consumption (kg/hr). Typical values are 4 to 8 for coal and 12 to 14 for oil. Direct Method (Input-Output Method): Formula: Î· = [Q Ã— (hs - hfw)] / [q Ã— GCV] Ã— 100 where Q = steam generation rate (kg/hr), hs = enthalpy of steam (kcal/kg), hfw = enthalpy of feed water (kcal/kg), q = fuel firing rate (kg/hr), GCV = Gross Calorific Value of fuel (kcal/kg). Advantages: Quick, easy, requires few measurements. Disadvantages: Does not tell you where the losses are, less accurate. Indirect Method (Heat Loss Method): Calculates all individual heat losses and subtracts from 100%. Formula: Î· = 100 - (sum of all percentage heat losses) Losses calculated: L1 (dry flue gas), L2 (hydrogen in fuel), L3 (moisture in fuel), L4 (moisture in air), L5 (CO loss), L6 (radiation and unaccounted), L7 (unburnt in fly ash), L8 (unburnt in bottom ash). Advantages: Tells exactly where losses occur, helps identify improvement areas. Disadvantages: Requires many measurements, time consuming.\r\n68: \r\n69: **Q: Calculate the efficiency of a boiler using indirect method for the following data. (7 marks)**\r\n70: \r\n71: General Formulas: Theoretical air = [11.6C + 34.8(H2 - O2/8) + 4.35S] / 100 kg/kg of fuel % Excess Air = O2% / (21 - O2%) Ã— 100 Actual air = Theoretical air Ã— (1 + EA/100) Given Data: Ultimate Analysis: Carbon = 84%, Hydrogen = 12%, Nitrogen = 0.5%, Oxygen = 1.5%, Sulphur = 1.5%, Moisture = 0.5% GCV of fuel = 10000 kcal/kg Fuel firing rate = 2648.125 kg/hr Surface temperature of boiler = 90Â°C Humidity = 0.025 kg/kg of dry air Theoretical air required = 13.92 kg/kg of oil Mass of dry flue gas = 21.36 kg/kg of oil Flue gas temperature = 190Â°C Ambient temperature = 30Â°C CO2% in flue gas = 10.8% O2% in flue gas = 7.4% Cp of flue gas = 0.23 kcal/kgÂ°C Cp of superheated steam or moisture = 0.45 kcal/kgÂ°C Solution: Step 1: Calculate Excess Air % Excess Air = (O2%) / (21 - O2%) Ã— 100 % Excess Air = 7.4 / (21 - 7.4) Ã— 100 % Excess Air = 7.4 / 13.6 Ã— 100 % Excess Air = 54.41% Step 2: Calculate Actual Air Supplied Actual mass of air supplied = Theoretical air Ã— (1 + EA/100) Actual mass of air supplied = 13.92 Ã— (1 + 54.41/100) Actual mass of air supplied = 13.92 Ã— 1.5441 Actual mass of air supplied = 21.49 kg/kg of fuel Step 3: Calculate Individual Losses L1 = Heat loss due to dry flue gas L1 = m_dfg Ã— Cp Ã— (Tf - Ta) / GCV Ã— 100 L1 = 21.36 Ã— 0.23 Ã— (190 - 30) / 10000 Ã— 100 L1 = 21.36 Ã— 0.23 Ã— 160 / 10000 Ã— 100 L1 = 785.088 / 10000 Ã— 100 L1 = 7.85% L2 = Heat loss due to hydrogen in fuel L2 = 9 Ã— H2 Ã— [584 + Cp_water Ã— (Tf - Ta)] / GCV Ã— 100 L2 = 9 Ã— 0.12 Ã— [584 + 0.45 Ã— (190 - 30)] / 10000 Ã— 100 L2 = 1.08 Ã— [584 + 72] / 10000 Ã— 100 L2 = 1.08 Ã— 656 / 10000 Ã— 100 L2 = 708.48 / 10000 Ã— 100 L2 = 7.08% L3 = Heat loss due to moisture in fuel L3 = M Ã— [584 + Cp_water Ã— (Tf - Ta)] / GCV Ã— 100 L3 = 0.005 Ã— [584 + 0.45 Ã— 160] / 10000 Ã— 100 L3 = 0.005 Ã— 656 / 10000 Ã— 100 L3 = 3.28 / 10000 Ã— 100 L3 = 0.03% L4 = Heat loss due to moisture in air L4 = AAS Ã— humidity Ã— Cp_water Ã— (Tf - Ta) / GCV Ã— 100 L4 = 21.49 Ã— 0.025 Ã— 0.45 Ã— 160 / 10000 Ã— 100 L4 = 38.682 / 10000 Ã— 100 L4 = 0.39% L5 = Heat loss due to CO Since CO is not given in the problem, assume L5 = 0% If CO is given, L5 = [CO / (CO + CO2)] Ã— C Ã— 5654 / GCV Ã— 100 L6 = Radiation and other unaccounted losses Assume L6 = 2.0% Step 4: Calculate Total Losses Total losses = L1 + L2 + L3 + L4 + L5 + L6 Total losses = 7.85 + 7.08 + 0.03 + 0.39 + 0 + 2.0 Total losses = 17.35% Step 5: Calculate Boiler Efficiency Boiler efficiency = 100 - Total losses Boiler efficiency = 100 - 17.35 Boiler efficiency = 82.65%\r\n72: \r\n73: **Q: What are steam traps? State their functions and benefits. (4 marks)**\r\n74: \r\n75: Definition: A steam trap is an automatic valve that discharges condensate, air, and non-condensable gases from a steam system while preventing the escape of live steam. Functions: 1. Remove condensate from steam lines and equipment as soon as it forms. 2. Remove air and non-condensable gases from the steam system. 3. Prevent live steam from escaping to conserve steam. Benefits: 1. Increases heat transfer efficiency because a condensate film on a heat transfer surface acts as an insulator. 2. Prevents water hammer caused by slugs of condensate in steam lines. 3. Reduces corrosion caused by air and CO2 in the steam system. 4. Saves energy by preventing steam loss. 5. Protects equipment from damage due to condensate accumulation.\r\n76: \r\n77: **Q: List out types of steam traps and explain any two with neat sketches. (7 marks)**\r\n78: \r\n79: Definition: A steam trap is an automatic valve that discharges condensate, air, and non-condensable gases from a steam system while preventing the escape of live steam. Functions: 1. Remove condensate from steam lines and equipment as soon as it forms. 2. Remove air and non-condensable gases from the steam system. 3. Prevent live steam from escaping to conserve steam. Benefits: 1. Increases heat transfer efficiency. 2. Prevents water hammer. 3. Reduces corrosion. 4. Saves energy by preventing steam loss. 5. Protects equipment from damage. Types of Steam Traps: 1. Mechanical traps work on the density difference between steam and condensate. Examples include the ball float trap and inverted bucket trap. 2. Thermostatic traps work on the temperature difference between steam and condensate. Examples include the balanced pressure trap and bimetallic trap. 3. Thermodynamic traps work on the difference in flow characteristics of steam and condensate. Examples include the disc trap. Explain Ball Float Trap: A hollow ball floats on condensate and sinks in steam. When condensate enters the trap body, the float rises and opens the valve, allowing condensate to drain. When steam enters, the float drops and closes the valve, trapping the steam. This trap gives continuous condensate discharge. [DIAGRAM: Ball float steam trap showing inlet, float ball, valve seat, outlet, and body] Explain Inverted Bucket Trap: An inverted bucket inside the trap body floats when steam enters it due to buoyancy. When steam enters the bucket, it floats up and closes the outlet valve. As steam condenses inside the bucket, the bucket sinks and opens the valve to discharge condensate. This trap operates intermittently. [DIAGRAM: Inverted bucket steam trap showing inverted bucket, inlet, outlet valve, and body]\r\n80: \r\n81: **Q: List factors affecting refrigeration and air conditioning system performance and explain any one. Explain techniques of energy conservation in refrigerated cold storage plants. (4 marks)**\r\n82: \r\n83: Factors affecting system performance: 1. Evaporator temperature and condition. Fouled coils reduce performance. 2. Condenser temperature and condition. A dirty condenser increases head pressure. 3. Refrigerant charge. Undercharge or overcharge both reduce COP. 4. Compressor efficiency and capacity control. 5. Insulation of cold rooms and chilled water pipes. 6. Air infiltration through doors and loading docks. 7. Heat load from lighting, people, and equipment inside the conditioned space. Explain condenser condition: If the condenser coils are dirty or fouled, heat rejection is poor. This increases the condensing pressure and temperature. For every 1Â°C increase in condensing temperature, power consumption of the compressor increases by about 3 to 3.5%. Regular cleaning of condenser coils can save 5 to 10% of energy.\r\n84: \r\n85: **Q: List the energy saving opportunities in refrigeration and air conditioning plant. (7 marks)**\r\n86: \r\n87: 1. Maintain proper condenser cleaning schedule. Dirty condensers increase compressor power by 3% per degree rise in condensing temperature. 2. Increase evaporator temperature. For every 1Â°C increase in evaporator temperature, COP improves by 2 to 3%. Do not cool below the required temperature. 3. Ensure proper refrigerant charge. Both undercharge and overcharge reduce efficiency. 4. Use variable frequency drives (VFDs) on compressors and fans for part-load operation. 5. Reduce air infiltration in cold rooms. Install strip curtains, air curtains, or rapid-closing doors. 6. Improve insulation on cold room walls, floors, and ceilings. Check for damaged insulation. 7. Reduce internal heat loads. Use LED lighting inside cold rooms instead of incandescent. Minimize door opening frequency. 8. Regular maintenance of expansion valves, filters, and oil levels. 9. Use economizer cycle or free cooling when ambient temperature is low enough. 10. Recover condenser waste heat for water heating or space heating. 11. Group similar temperature products together. Avoid mixing products with different temperature requirements. 12. Proper sizing of equipment. Oversized systems cycle on and off frequently, wasting energy.\r\n88: \r\n89: **Q: Explain blow down, its necessity, and advantages. (4 marks)**\r\n90: \r\n91: Blow down is the process of removing a portion of boiler water from the drum to reduce the concentration of dissolved solids, chemicals, and other impurities. Necessity: As water evaporates into steam in the boiler, dissolved solids remain behind and their concentration keeps increasing. If not removed, these concentrated impurities cause scale formation on heat transfer surfaces, foaming and carryover of water into steam, and corrosion of boiler tubes. Blow down keeps the total dissolved solids (TDS) within acceptable limits. Types: 1. Intermittent blow down or bottom blow down. Done manually at intervals. Removes sludge and heavy sediment from the bottom of the boiler drum. Takes about 10 to 15 seconds each time. 2. Continuous blow down. Water is continuously removed from the point of highest TDS concentration. Flow rate is controlled by a valve. Allows heat recovery from blow down water. Advantages: 1. Prevents scale formation on boiler tubes. Scale even 1mm thick can increase fuel consumption by 5 to 8%. 2. Reduces foaming and carryover of water droplets in steam. 3. Prevents corrosion of boiler tubes and drum. 4. Maintains water quality within prescribed limits. 5. Continuous blow down heat can be recovered using a flash tank and heat exchanger.\r\n92: \r\n93: **Q: Discuss the energy conservation opportunities for boiler in detail. (7 marks)**\r\n94: \r\n95: 1. Reduce excess air: Monitor and control excess air using O2 analyzers. For every 1% reduction in excess air, fuel savings of about 0.5% can be achieved. Typical excess air is 15 to 20% for oil and 20 to 30% for coal. 2. Install economizer: Recovers heat from flue gases to preheat boiler feed water. For every 6Â°C increase in feed water temperature, fuel savings of about 1% can be achieved. 3. Install air preheater: Uses flue gas heat to preheat combustion air. For every 20Â°C rise in combustion air temperature, there is about 1% fuel saving. 4. Minimize blow down: Excessive blow down wastes energy. Use automatic TDS controllers. Recover heat from blow down water using flash tanks. 5. Improve boiler insulation: Check and repair damaged insulation on boiler shell, steam pipes, and valves. A bare steam pipe at 200Â°C can lose 1000 kcal/hr per meter length. 6. Recover condensate: Return hot condensate to the boiler feed water tank. Condensate at 90Â°C contains about 50 kcal/kg of heat that would otherwise be wasted. 7. Optimize boiler loading: Operate boiler at 65 to 85% of rated capacity for best efficiency. Very low loads below 25% and overloading both reduce efficiency. 8. Improve feed water treatment: Scale on boiler tubes acts as an insulator. Even 1mm of calcium scale can increase fuel consumption by 5 to 8%. Proper softening and deaeration of feed water prevents scale. 9. Reduce steam leaks: A small leak of 3mm diameter on a 7 kg/cm2 steam line can waste about 30 tons of steam per year. 10. Use waste heat from flue gases: If flue gas exit temperature is above 200Â°C, there is significant scope for heat recovery. 11. Regular soot blowing: Soot deposits on boiler tubes act as an insulator and reduce heat transfer. Regular soot blowing maintains heat transfer efficiency. 12. Monitor and maintain steam traps: Failed steam traps can cause significant steam and condensate losses.\r\n96: \r\n97: **Q: Discuss various losses in fuel-fired furnaces in detail. (7 marks)**\r\n98: \r\n99: 1. Heat loss through flue gases or stack loss: This is the largest loss, typically 30 to 40% of total heat input. Hot flue gases carry away sensible heat. Loss depends on flue gas temperature and excess air. Higher flue gas temperature results in higher loss. In a well-designed furnace, this should be minimized to 20 to 25%. 2. Heat loss due to incomplete combustion: If combustion is incomplete, CO is formed instead of CO2. CO has a calorific value of 2400 kcal/Nm3 that is wasted. This is caused by insufficient air, poor mixing, or low combustion temperature. 3. Heat loss through furnace walls or wall loss: Heat is conducted through furnace walls and radiated from outer surfaces. This depends on insulation thickness, wall temperature, and furnace surface area. Typically 2 to 5% of total input. 4. Heat loss due to opening in furnace: Every time the furnace door opens, hot gases escape and cold air enters. Radiation heat loss through openings is proportional to the fourth power of temperature. Keep doors closed and properly sealed. 5. Heat stored in furnace structure: During heating, the furnace walls, hearth, and roof absorb heat. This is a loss during batch operations because stored heat is wasted during the cooling period. Use low thermal mass materials like ceramic fiber to reduce this loss. 6. Heat loss due to water cooling: In furnaces with water-cooled components like doors, frames, and skid pipes, heat is removed by cooling water. This heat is usually not recovered. 7. Heat loss due to scale formation: Oxidation of the metal surface forms scale. Scale formation represents material loss and the heat absorbed by the oxidation process. 8. Heat carried away by the heated material or useful heat: This is not actually a loss, it is the useful output. However, if the product is overheated beyond requirement, the extra heat is wasted.\r\n100: \r\n101: **Q: Explain deaeration treatment for boiler water. (3 marks)**\r\n102: \r\n103: Deaeration is the process of removing dissolved gases, mainly oxygen and carbon dioxide, from boiler feed water before it enters the boiler. Why it is needed: Dissolved oxygen causes pitting corrosion on boiler tubes and drum. CO2 forms carbonic acid which causes acidic corrosion. Even small amounts like 0.1 ppm of O2 can cause significant damage over time. How it works: Feed water is heated to its saturation temperature in a deaerator vessel. At saturation temperature, the solubility of gases in water drops to near zero, and dissolved gases are released. These gases are vented out through a vent condenser at the top of the deaerator. The deaerator also acts as a feed water heater, raising the water temperature to 105 to 110Â°C. Types: 1. Spray type deaerator. 2. Tray type deaerator.\r\n104: \r\n105: **Q: What is CDM? Write a note on CDM methodology and its project cycle with neat sketch. Explain the objectives of CDM and how CDM promotes sustainable development. (7 marks)**\r\n106: \r\n107: CDM is one of the three flexible mechanisms defined in the Kyoto Protocol under Article 12. It allows developed countries to invest in emission reducing projects in developing countries and earn Certified Emission Reductions (CERs) or carbon credits. Each CER equals one tonne of CO2 equivalent not emitted. Objectives: 1. Help developing countries achieve sustainable development. 2. Help developed countries meet their emission reduction targets cost effectively. 3. Transfer clean technology and financial resources to developing countries. CDM Project Cycle: 1. Project Design: The project developer prepares a Project Design Document (PDD) describing the project activity, baseline methodology, monitoring plan, and expected emission reductions. 2. National Approval: The project must be approved by the Designated National Authority (DNA) of the host country. In India, this is the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change. DNA confirms the project contributes to sustainable development. 3. Validation: An independent Designated Operational Entity (DOE) accredited by the CDM Executive Board reviews the PDD and validates whether it meets CDM requirements. 4. Registration: The DOE submits the validated PDD to the CDM Executive Board for formal registration. Registration is the official acceptance of the project as a CDM project. 5. Monitoring: The project developer monitors the emission reductions as per the monitoring plan in the PDD. Monitoring data is collected throughout the crediting period. 6. Verification: A second independent DOE verifies the monitored emission reductions. This DOE must be different from the one that did validation. 7. Issuance of CERs: After successful verification, the CDM Executive Board issues CERs for the verified emission reductions. These CERs can be traded in the carbon market. [DIAGRAM NEEDED: CDM Project Cycle flowchart showing Project Design (PDD) to National Approval (DNA) to Validation (DOE) to Registration (CDM Executive Board) to Monitoring (Project Developer) to Verification (Second DOE) to CER Issuance (CDM Executive Board). Show as a vertical or circular flowchart with arrows between each step.] Examples of CDM projects in India include wind power projects, solar power, biomass power, energy efficiency improvement in industries, and methane recovery from landfills. Benefits to developing countries include technology transfer, foreign investment, employment, and sustainable development. Benefits to developed countries include cost effective emission reduction and meeting Kyoto targets.\r\n108: \r\n109: **Q: How does the Bachat Lamp Yojna work? (3 marks)**\r\n110: \r\n111: Bachat Lamp Yojna (BLY) was launched by the Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) in 2009. It is a CDM based scheme for distributing CFL and later LED bulbs to households across India at subsidized rates. Under this scheme, BEE partners with private companies called Implementing Entities. These companies distribute CFL or LED bulbs to households by collecting old incandescent bulbs. The company recovers its cost through the sale of CERs generated by the energy savings from replacing inefficient bulbs. Households get efficient bulbs at a price of Rs 15 instead of Rs 80 to 100.\r\n112: \r\n113: **Q: Explain Bachat Lamp Yojana, its aim and benefits. (7 marks)**\r\n114: \r\n115: Aim: The aim is to reduce electricity consumption of the domestic lighting sector by replacing inefficient incandescent bulbs with energy efficient CFL or LED bulbs while using the CDM carbon credit mechanism to fund the subsidy. Scheme Design: 1. BEE acts as the coordinating agency. 2. Private Implementing Entities (IEs) are selected through competitive bidding. 3. IEs distribute CFLs or LEDs to households at highly subsidized prices. 4. Old incandescent bulbs are collected and destroyed to prevent reuse. 5. The energy savings from replacing bulbs qualify as a CDM project. 6. IEs register the project with UNFCCC and earn CERs. 7. Revenue from selling CERs in the international carbon market covers the subsidy cost. 8. Each CFL replacing a 60W incandescent bulb saves about 45W. Operating 4 hours per day saves 65 kWh per year. Benefits: 1. Reduces household electricity bills by 40 to 50 percent on lighting. 2. Reduces peak electricity demand since lighting accounts for 20 percent of India's peak demand. 3. Reduces CO2 emissions as each CFL saves about 40 kg CO2 per year. 4. Costs the government nothing since the scheme is self financing through CERs. 5. Generates employment in distribution and monitoring. 6. Promotes awareness about energy efficiency in households. 7. Reduces the need for building new power plants to meet lighting demand. The scheme originally targeted distributing 400 million CFLs across India. Later, the focus shifted from CFLs to LED bulbs under the UJALA scheme launched in 2015.\r\n116: \r\n117: **Q: Discuss the significance of UNFCCC in addressing global climate change challenges. (4 marks)**\r\n118: \r\n119: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. Currently, 198 countries have ratified the Convention. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system. The Convention follows the principle of \"common but differentiated responsibilities\" meaning all countries share the responsibility of fighting climate change, but developed countries should take the lead since they have historically contributed more to greenhouse gas emissions.\r\n120: \r\n121: **Q: Write a short note on climate change and development of UNFCCC. (7 marks)**\r\n122: \r\n123: UNFCCC stands for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change. It is an international environmental treaty adopted at the Earth Summit in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in June 1992. It came into force on 21 March 1994. The main objective of UNFCCC is to stabilize greenhouse gas concentrations in the atmosphere at a level that prevents dangerous human interference with the climate system. Key principles of UNFCCC: 1. Common but differentiated responsibilities: Developed countries should lead in reducing emissions. 2. Precautionary principle: Lack of full scientific certainty should not be used as a reason to delay action. 3. Right to sustainable development: Countries have the right to promote sustainable development. 4. Supportive and open international economic system: Trade measures should not be used as barriers. Classification of countries: 1. Annex I: Developed countries and economies in transition including USA, EU, Japan, Russia, and Australia. 2. Annex II: Subset of Annex I wealthy developed countries required to provide financial and technical support to developing countries. 3. Non-Annex I: Developing countries including India, China, and Brazil. Major outcomes and agreements under UNFCCC: 1. Kyoto Protocol (1997): First legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries. 2. Copenhagen Accord (2009): Voluntary pledges. 3. Paris Agreement (2015): All countries agreed to limit warming to 2Â°C with Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs). UNFCCC operates through annual meetings called Conference of Parties (COP). The COP is the supreme decision making body of the Convention. UNFCCC created the framework for all subsequent climate agreements. Without UNFCCC, there would be no Kyoto Protocol, no Paris Agreement, and no CDM mechanism.\r\n124: \r\n125: **Q: Explain the principles and objectives of the Kyoto Protocol. Assess the effectiveness of the Kyoto Protocol in reducing greenhouse gas emissions. (4 marks)**\r\n126: \r\n127: The Kyoto Protocol is an international agreement linked to UNFCCC that was adopted in Kyoto, Japan on 11 December 1997 and came into force on 16 February 2005. Key features: 1. Set legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries. First commitment period was 2008 to 2012 to reduce GHG emissions by an average of 5.2 percent below 1990 levels. Second commitment period was 2013 to 2020 via Doha Amendment. 2. Covers six greenhouse gases: CO2, CH4, N2O, HFCs, PFCs, SF6. 3. Has three flexible mechanisms to help countries meet targets: CDM, Joint Implementation, and Emissions Trading. 4. Developing countries including India and China have no binding targets. 5. USA never ratified the Protocol and Canada withdrew in 2012. Effectiveness: The Protocol succeeded in reducing emissions in many developed countries. The EU met their collective target. However, global emissions continued to rise because major emitters like USA, China, and India were not bound by targets. This led to the Paris Agreement in 2015 which covers all countries.\r\n128: \r\n129: **Q: Explain Sustainable development. (3 marks)**\r\n130: \r\n131: Sustainable development is defined as development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. It rests on three pillars: economic growth, social inclusion, and environmental protection. In the context of energy, sustainable development means using energy resources efficiently, switching to renewable sources, and reducing pollution and greenhouse gas emissions while maintaining economic growth.\r\n132: \r\n133: **Q: What is the Prototype Carbon Fund? What are its objectives? State the three primary strategic objectives of PCF. (3 marks)**\r\n134: \r\n135: The Prototype Carbon Fund (PCF) was launched by the World Bank in April 2000 with capital of US$ 180 million contributed by 6 governments and 17 private companies. PCF was the first carbon fund in the world. It was designed to demonstrate how project based greenhouse gas emission reductions under the Kyoto Protocol CDM and JI mechanisms could work in practice. Three primary objectives of PCF: 1. To show how project based transactions for greenhouse gas emission reductions can contribute to sustainable development and lower the cost of compliance with Kyoto Protocol targets. 2. To provide a learning experience for stakeholders on how the CDM and JI mechanisms work in practice. 3. To demonstrate how the World Bank can work as a financial intermediary in the carbon market, acting as trustee of the fund and purchasing emission reductions on behalf of contributors. PCF invested in 23 projects in developing countries and economies in transition covering renewable energy, energy efficiency, and waste management.\r\n136: \r\n137: **Q: State the roles and responsibilities of COP (Conference of the Parties). (4 marks)**\r\n138: \r\n139: COP stands for Conference of the Parties. It is the supreme decision making body of the UNFCCC. All countries that have ratified the Convention are represented at COP. COP meets annually in different cities around the world. Roles and responsibilities: 1. Review the implementation of the Convention and its related legal instruments. 2. Make decisions necessary to promote effective implementation of the Convention. 3. Adopt new protocols, amendments, or annexes to the Convention. 4. Review national communications and emission inventories submitted by Parties. 5. Assess the overall effects of measures taken by Parties to address climate change. 6. Mobilize financial resources for climate action in developing countries. 7. Review reports from subsidiary bodies. Notable COPs include COP 3 in Kyoto for the Kyoto Protocol, COP 15 in Copenhagen for the Copenhagen Accord, COP 21 in Paris for the Paris Agreement, and COP 26 in Glasgow for the Glasgow Climate Pact.\r\n140: \r\n141: **Q: Explain the Carbon Credit Trade Scheme. (3-4 marks)**\r\n142: \r\n143: A carbon credit is a tradable permit or certificate that allows the holder to emit one tonne of CO2 or an equivalent amount of another greenhouse gas. How the scheme works: 1. A regulatory body sets a cap on total emissions allowed. 2. Emission allowances are distributed or auctioned to industries. 3. Companies that reduce their emissions below their allowance have surplus credits. 4. Companies that emit more than their allowance must buy credits from those with surplus. 5. This creates a market based mechanism where the price of carbon is determined by supply and demand. 6. The system rewards companies that invest in clean technology and penalizes those that continue polluting. Types of carbon markets include compliance markets created by mandatory regulations and voluntary markets where companies voluntarily buy carbon credits to offset their emissions. India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) was notified in June 2023 under the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022. BEE is the administrator. It covers obligated entities in designated sectors. India plans to have a fully operational carbon market by 2026.\r\n144: \r\n145: **Q: Explain the Perform Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme. (3-4 marks)**\r\n146: \r\n147: PAT is a market based energy efficiency trading mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency. This is one of the eight missions under India's National Action Plan on Climate Change. How PAT works: 1. BEE identifies Designated Consumers (DCs) in energy intensive sectors such as thermal power plants, iron and steel, cement, aluminum, fertilizers, textile, pulp and paper, chlor alkali, railways, refineries, petrochemicals, distribution companies, and buildings. 2. Each DC is given a Specific Energy Consumption (SEC) reduction target based on their current performance. 3. DCs that reduce their SEC beyond the target earn Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts). 4. DCs that fail to meet their target must buy ESCerts from the market or pay a penalty. 5. ESCerts are traded on two power exchanges: Indian Energy Exchange and Power Exchange India Limited. PAT Cycle I covered 478 designated consumers and achieved energy savings of 8.67 million tonnes of oil equivalent against a target of 6.686 MTOE. This exceeded the target by about 30 percent. PAT is currently in its 7th cycle, covering over 1000 designated consumers across 13 sectors.\r\n148: \r\n149: **Question: Write a note on CDM methodology and its project cycle with neat sketch. (7 marks)**\r\n150: \r\n151: **What to draw:** A vertical or circular flowchart with 7 boxes connected by arrows: Project Design (PDD) -> National Approval (DNA) -> Validation (DOE) -> Registration (CDM Executive Board) -> Monitoring (Project Developer) -> Verification (Second DOE) -> CER Issuance (CDM Executive Board) **Google search:** `CDM project cycle flowchart diagram`\r\n152: \r\n153: **Question: Explain ball float steam trap with neat sketch. (Part of 7-mark steam trap question)**\r\n154: \r\n155: **What to draw:** Cross-section showing: trap body (chamber), inlet pipe on left, outlet pipe on right-bottom, a hollow ball floating on condensate, valve seat connected to the ball by a lever, vent at top for air release. **How it works in the diagram:** When condensate fills the body, ball rises and opens valve. When steam enters, ball drops and closes valve. **Google search:** `ball float steam trap working diagram`\r\n156: \r\n157: **Question: Explain inverted bucket steam trap with neat sketch. (Part of 7-mark steam trap question)**\r\n158: \r\n159: **What to draw:** Cross-section showing: trap body, an inverted bucket (open end down) inside the body, inlet pipe entering at bottom into the bucket, outlet valve at top connected to bucket by a lever, outlet pipe. **How it works in the diagram:** Steam enters bucket from below, bucket floats up, closes valve. Steam condenses, bucket sinks, valve opens, condensate drains out. **Google search:** `inverted bucket steam trap working diagram`\r\n160: \r\n161: **Question: This is not a specific PYQ diagram, but drawing a pie chart or bar chart of boiler losses helps in the indirect method question.**\r\n162: \r\n163: **What to draw:** A simple pie chart showing typical losses for an oil-fired boiler: - Dry flue gas loss: 7-8% - Hydrogen in fuel: 7% - Moisture losses: 0.5% - Radiation: 2% - Useful heat output: 82-83% *End of Study Guide*\r\n164: \r\n165: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
