{"step_index":14,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:30:06Z","content":"**1 Burns Classification Pathophysiology and Comprehensive Management** **Definition** A burn is an acute traumatic injury to the skin subcutaneous tissues or deeper structures caused by thermal energy flame scald contact chemical agents electricity or radiation resulting in protein denaturation cellular necrosis and systemic burn shock **Classification by Depth of Injury** **1st Degree Superficial** • Anatomy Epidermis only • Clinical Appearance Erythematous dry no blisters • Sensation Hyperesthetic painful • Healing / Intervention Heals in 3 6 days no scarring **2nd Degree Superficial Partial** • Anatomy Epidermis and Papillary Dermis • Clinical Appearance Pink / red moist thin walled bullae brisk refill • Sensation Intensely painful • Healing / Intervention Heals in 7 14 days minimal scar **2nd Degree Deep Partial** • Anatomy Epidermis and Reticular Dermis • Clinical Appearance Mottled white / red waxy dry sluggish refill • Sensation Dull pinprick sensation • Healing / Intervention Heals in 3 8 weeks with hypertrophic scar needs excision and grafting **3rd Degree Full Thickness** • Anatomy Entire Epidermis and Dermis • Clinical Appearance Leathery porcelain white / charred non blanching • Sensation Insensate anesthetic • Healing / Intervention Requires surgical excision and skin grafting **4th Degree Subdermal** • Anatomy Extends into fat fascia muscle bone • Clinical Appearance Charred blackened exposed bone / muscle • Sensation Anesthetic • Healing / Intervention Debridement flap coverage or amputation **Pathophysiology of Burn Injury** • Jacksons Zones of Burn Injury • Zone of Coagulation Central Point of maximum damage irreversible protein denaturation and tissue necrosis • Zone of Stasis Middle Compromised tissue perfusion and ischemia salvageable with adequate fluid resuscitation prone to secondary necrosis if untreated • Zone of Hyperemia Outer Peripheral vasodilation due to inflammatory mediator release viable tissue that recovers spontaneously • Systemic Burn Shock and SIRS TBSA greater than 20 25 percent Widespread release of histamine bradykinin prostaglandins and oxygen radicals causes massive microvascular hyperpermeability Intravascular fluid and albumin leak into the interstitium causing profound hypovolemia hemoconcentration and hypermetabolism resting energy expenditure increases up to 200 percent **Burn Size Estimation TBSA** • Wallace Rule of Nines Adults Head and Neck 9 percent Each Arm 9 percent 18 percent total Anterior Trunk 18 percent Posterior Trunk 18 percent Each Leg 18 percent 36 percent total Perineum 1 percent • Lund Browder Chart Gold standard for pediatrics accounts for disproportionately large head and smaller limbs in children • Palmar Surface Rule Patients open hand palm and fingers approximately 1 percent TBSA **Acute Resuscitation Protocol** **Parkland Baxter Formula** Total 24 hour Fluid Ringers Lactate equals 4 mL times Body Weight kg times percent TBSA • First 8 Hours Administer 50 percent of the calculated fluid measured from the exact time of burn injury • Next 16 Hours Administer the remaining 50 percent of the total fluid volume Updated ABA Consensus Recommends 2 mL / kg / percent TBSA for adult thermal burns to avoid over resuscitation fluid creep **Monitoring Endpoints** Target hourly urine output of 0 5 mL / kg / hr in adults 30 50 mL / hr and 1 0 mL / kg / hr in children For electrical burns with myoglobinuria maintain 1 5 2 0 mL / kg / hr until clear **Surgical Management** 